<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SlimDep.Analysis\SlimDep.Analysis.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SlimDep.Core\SlimDep.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="System.CommandLine" Version="2.0.9" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="SlimDep.UnitTests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- Include README and icon in the NuGet package -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="..\..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
    <None Include="..\..\icon.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <AssemblyName>slimdep</AssemblyName>

    <!-- Tool settings -->
    <PackAsTool>true</PackAsTool>
    <ToolCommandName>slimdep</ToolCommandName>

    <!-- Package identity -->
    <PackageId>SlimDep</PackageId>
    <Version>1.2.0</Version>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://www.nuget.org/packages/SlimDep</PackageProjectUrl>
    <Description>Detect and remove unused NuGet package references in .NET projects.</Description>
    <PackageTags>nuget;dotnet-tool;unused;dependencies;packages;csproj;analyzer;cleanup;remove;detect;roslyn;static-analysis;sarif;package-reference;unused-packages;dependency-cleanup;dotnet;tool;net8;slim;nuget-cleanup;unused-nuget;dependency-analyzer;github-code-scanning</PackageTags>
    <PackageIcon>icon.png</PackageIcon>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageReleaseNotes>v1.2.0 — More trustworthy results.

New features:
- SLIMDEP002: flags transitive packages your code uses directly without a &lt;PackageReference&gt; (they can vanish when the package that brings them in changes). Ignores framework-provided namespaces, namespaces shared with direct packages, SDK implicit usings, project references and metapackages (e.g. xunit). Informational by default; --fail-on-undeclared makes it fail CI. Reported in console, JSON (undeclaredPackages) and SARIF (rule SLIMDEP002, level note).
- Version-pin detection: a direct reference that raises the version of a transitive dependency (typically a security pin) is kept and listed as a pin instead of being reported unused, so fix no longer downgrades it.
- Built-in safe list: test adapters, coverage collectors, SourceLink, versioning, design-time tools, analyzers, runtime-loaded providers and PrivateAssets="all" references are never reported as unused. Skipped packages are listed; --no-default-ignores disables the list.
- JSON output gains undeclaredPackages, pinnedPackages and skippedPackages arrays.

v1.1.1 — Bug-fix release.

Fixes:
- --include-transitive now works: unused transitive packages are reported (marked transitive, with the reference that brings them in); fix lists but never removes them
- --format json / sarif to stdout now emit only the report (progress lines no longer corrupt it); multi-project JSON is a single array
- Multi-targeted projects: every target framework is analysed, and usings inside #if blocks are counted
- SDK implicit usings, &lt;Using&gt; items (csproj / Directory.Build.*) and generated GlobalUsings.g.cs are honoured; avoids removing e.g. Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting from Worker projects
- fix keeps formatting, comments, line endings, BOM and XML declaration; keeps conditional or commented ItemGroups; reports packages it could not find in the project file; exits 2 on errors
- Legacy MSBuild xmlns projects and global:: qualified names handled
- SARIF tool version now comes from the assembly version
- Invalid --format values are rejected instead of silently falling back to console
- Implicit SDK references (autoReferenced) are never reported

v1.1.0 — SARIF output support.

New features:
- SARIF 2.1.0 output format (--format sarif): machine-readable static analysis results consumed natively by GitHub Code Scanning and Azure DevOps, enabling inline PR annotations with zero pipeline scripting
- Line number detection: each SARIF result now points to the exact line of the unused &lt;PackageReference&gt; element in the .csproj file
- Multi-project SARIF: scanning a folder emits a single well-formed SARIF document (one run, all findings, all projects listed as artifacts)

Usage:
  slimdep scan . --format sarif
  slimdep scan . --format sarif --output results.sarif

Breaking changes: none

v1.0.1 — Patch release.
- Fixed Supported Features table rendering on NuGet.org (CRLF → LF line endings in README)

v1.0.0 features:
- Fully-qualified name detection: packages used via Foo.Bar.Type syntax (no using directive) are no longer false-positives
- .slimdepignore support: add a .slimdepignore file to suppress specific packages from analysis
- --ignore flag: suppress packages inline without a file (slimdep scan . --ignore Pkg1 --ignore Pkg2)
- slimdep ignore command: manage .slimdepignore from the CLI (add / remove / list)
- Central Package Management (CPM) support: GlobalPackageReference entries are automatically excluded
- XAML namespace analysis: xmlns clr-namespace declarations in .xaml files (WPF, MAUI) are now scanned
- Razor / CSHTML namespace analysis: @using directives in .razor and .cshtml files (Blazor, MVC) are now scanned
- Parallel multi-project scanning: all projects scanned concurrently (bounded by CPU count)</PackageReleaseNotes>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
